const orders = db.orders.find({ userId: 1 }).toArray(); // extra round-trips
// Re-implementing joins in code throws away what a relational database does best.
```

```java
// Add dependency: spring-boot-starter-data-redis

@Service
public class ProductService {
    private final ProductRepository repository;

    @Cacheable(value = "products", key = "#id")
    public Optional<Product> findById(Long id) {
        // Expensive database query - Redis caches the result
        return repository.findById(id);
    }

    @CacheEvict(value = "products", key = "#product.id")
    public Product update(Product product) {
        return repository.save(product);
    }
}

@Configuration
@EnableCaching
public class RedisConfig {
    @Bean
    public RedisCacheManager cacheManager(RedisConnectionFactory factory) {
        return RedisCacheManager.builder(factory)
            .cacheDefaults(RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig()
                .entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(10)))  // Cache expires after 10 minutes
            .build();
    }
}
```

```sql
-- Atomic transaction: both account updates commit or neither does.
START TRANSACTION;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;
```
Whether another transaction sees old or new values, and whether a replica lags, depends on its isolation and replication path. A BASE-style system is not represented by generic SQL syntax; its write and convergence rules must come from the selected database.

```sql
-- A crash between these two statements loses money
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
```

```sql
-- Both updates commit together, or neither does.
START TRANSACTION;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;
```

Atomicity protects this transaction boundary. Read visibility, concurrent behavior, replica lag, and failure recovery still depend on the database configuration.

```sql
-- PostgreSQL JSONB: a GIN index can support this containment predicate.
CREATE INDEX idx_users_data ON users USING GIN (data);
SELECT * FROM users WHERE data @> '{"tier": "gold"}';

-- MySQL: expose one JSON path as a generated column and index it.
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN tier VARCHAR(20)
  GENERATED ALWAYS AS (JSON_UNQUOTE(JSON_EXTRACT(data, '$.tier'))) STORED;
CREATE INDEX idx_users_tier ON users (tier);
SELECT * FROM users WHERE tier = 'gold';
```

The syntax and supported plans are product- and version-specific. Verify the real query with `EXPLAIN`.
